1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,461
Why Underwriting Exists.

2
00:00:02,461 --> 00:00:08,436
Dash-fi extends high-limit charge cards
with no personal guarantee, up to 3%

3
00:00:08,436 --> 00:00:14,410
cashback, depending on a company's
financial profile, up to 30 days of float

4
00:00:14,410 --> 00:00:20,146
(in some cases 60 days for shipping
cards) and come with value-add agents

5
00:00:20,146 --> 00:00:26,360
that can analyze and audit your spend for
even more savings. These modules will

6
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:32,653
focus on our approach to underwriting and
the type of prospects Dash-fi is built

7
00:00:32,653 --> 00:00:32,972
for.

8
00:00:32,972 --> 00:00:35,666
So who is Dash-fi built for?

9
00:00:35,666 --> 00:00:39,938
Think about three types of companies that
choose to spend on Dash-fi.

10
00:00:39,938 --> 00:00:46,852
The first is an e-commerce or B2C brand
spending heavily on advertising — Meta,

11
00:00:46,852 --> 00:00:53,677
Google, TikTok, Amazon. They need high
daily limits because they spend tens of

12
00:00:53,677 --> 00:01:00,768
thousands of dollars on ad platforms all
for driving traffic to their website. If

13
00:01:00,768 --> 00:01:07,327
you're spending $30K or $200K a day on
ads, you likely need a card that can

14
00:01:07,327 --> 00:01:11,936
handle that volume and earn cashback on
every dollar.

15
00:01:11,936 --> 00:01:18,387
The second is a company with significant
shipping costs — FedEx, UPS, USPS, DHL,

16
00:01:18,387 --> 00:01:25,002
Stamps.com, and Amazon Shipping. They're
already spending that money. The question

17
00:01:25,002 --> 00:01:31,208
is whether they're earning 2.2% cashback
on Net-1, or nothing at all. In some

18
00:01:31,208 --> 00:01:36,271
cases we can offer up to 3% but more on
that in another module.

19
00:01:36,271 --> 00:01:42,114
The third is a company making large
supplier or vendor payments — inventory,

20
00:01:42,114 --> 00:01:48,192
raw materials, suppliers. These companies
choose Dash because they want to move

21
00:01:48,192 --> 00:01:54,113
payments onto a card to earn cashback on
spend that's already happening every

22
00:01:54,113 --> 00:01:55,126
single month.

23
00:01:55,126 --> 00:02:00,591
What connects all three of these
companies? Profitability — or a clear,

24
00:02:00,591 --> 00:02:06,056
demonstrable path to it. That's the
financial profile that allows us to

25
00:02:06,056 --> 00:02:12,301
extend the limits, the cashback, and the
float that make our card worth using. In

26
00:02:12,301 --> 00:02:18,391
this module, we'll go over what exactly
profitability means and how to evaluate

27
00:02:18,391 --> 00:02:18,625
it.

28
00:02:18,625 --> 00:02:25,314
Here's an example of the risk Dash is
taking on when we issue a card. Imagine a

29
00:02:25,314 --> 00:02:32,347
prospect of yours just got approved for a
$500K charge card on Net-15. Net-15 means

30
00:02:32,347 --> 00:02:39,122
every dollar spent on day one is repaid
on day 15 — and that's business days, so

31
00:02:39,122 --> 00:02:45,897
it doesn't include Saturdays and Sundays.
Day two's spend repays on day 16. It's

32
00:02:45,897 --> 00:02:52,757
rolling. And for those 15 business days,
Dash-fi is carrying that exposure. We're

33
00:02:52,757 --> 00:02:57,217
taking a position on that company's
ability to repay.

34
00:02:57,217 --> 00:03:03,265
What does a credit card issuer like Amex,
Capital One, and other traditional

35
00:03:03,265 --> 00:03:08,749
providers, do to avoid this kind of
exposure? They do it by extending

36
00:03:08,749 --> 00:03:14,717
significantly lower card limits. A
company doing $5M a month in revenue may

37
00:03:14,717 --> 00:03:20,927
only get a $100K limit on a competing
business card. Why, well lots of reasons

38
00:03:20,927 --> 00:03:26,975
actually. The company may have been at a
fraction of their current revenue 6

39
00:03:26,975 --> 00:03:33,588
months prior and growing rapidly. But No,
to traditional providers, what matters is

40
00:03:33,588 --> 00:03:37,620
their credit score and the perceived risk
exposure.

41
00:03:37,620 --> 00:03:40,360
And that's where the difference lies...

42
00:03:40,360 --> 00:03:46,577
To an aggressively growing company... a
low limit that is not aligned to their

43
00:03:46,577 --> 00:03:51,180
business spend and can ultimately become
a risk in itself.

44
00:03:51,180 --> 00:03:56,809
For our competition, a low limit is
scaled to the risk the issuer is willing

45
00:03:56,809 --> 00:03:57,635
to take on.

46
00:03:57,635 --> 00:04:03,585
But at Dash-fi we choose to take on that
exposure because our underwriting gives

47
00:04:03,585 --> 00:04:09,460
us an inside look at how that business
actually performs — their revenue, their

48
00:04:09,460 --> 00:04:15,184
cash position, their trajectory. That's
what allows us to extend a limit that

49
00:04:15,184 --> 00:04:18,347
matches the business, not one that caps
it.

50
00:04:18,347 --> 00:04:23,532
We work with brands spending rapidly
because that spend is the fuel that is

51
00:04:23,532 --> 00:04:29,207
powering their growth, and we'll happily
take a chance on those brands, as long as

52
00:04:29,207 --> 00:04:34,462
they meet our financial requirements
which we'll go over in our next module.

53
00:04:34,462 --> 00:04:40,075
The other advantage to our underwriting
is that it's not a one-time review. We

54
00:04:40,075 --> 00:04:45,396
frequently revisit the limits as a
company's financial profile changes. As

55
00:04:45,396 --> 00:04:51,082
their business grows and requires more —
or different — financial tools, we can

56
00:04:51,082 --> 00:04:52,759
extend limits to match.

57
00:04:52,759 --> 00:04:58,117
That flexibility isn't straight forward
with traditional card provider. Going

58
00:04:58,117 --> 00:05:03,474
back to Amex, Capital One, and Chase —
these corporate card providers may not

59
00:05:03,474 --> 00:05:09,044
just underwrite the business. They may
pull the founder's personal credit — FICO

60
00:05:09,044 --> 00:05:14,472
score, personal guarantee. Your mortgage,
your car payment, your student loans

61
00:05:14,472 --> 00:05:19,971
factor into whether the business gets
approved. That has nothing to do with how

62
00:05:19,971 --> 00:05:22,156
the business actually performs.

63
00:05:22,156 --> 00:05:28,486
If your prospect is looking for float —
extended repayment terms — we can extend

64
00:05:28,486 --> 00:05:34,896
Net-7, Net-15, or Net-30, depending on a
company's financial profile. Which we'll

65
00:05:34,896 --> 00:05:37,620
go over later on in this training.

66
00:05:37,620 --> 00:05:43,160
This is why underwriting matters. It's
the mechanism that determines what terms

67
00:05:43,160 --> 00:05:44,864
a company qualifies for.

68
00:05:44,864 --> 00:05:50,530
The underwriting team doesn't see their
work as pass or fail. It's a review of

69
00:05:50,530 --> 00:05:55,975
several financial indicators — cash
position, revenue trajectory, net asset

70
00:05:55,975 --> 00:06:01,199
position, profitability — that we'll
cover throughout this training. The

71
00:06:01,199 --> 00:06:07,086
stronger a company's financials, the more
options they have — higher limits, more

72
00:06:07,086 --> 00:06:12,457
float choices, and a broader range of
terms to choose from. A company with

73
00:06:12,457 --> 00:06:17,976
thinner financials still qualifies — at
terms that match their risk profile.

74
00:06:17,976 --> 00:06:23,599
We are the party on the hook if a
prospect can't repay. The terms reflect

75
00:06:23,599 --> 00:06:29,535
the risk profile the prospect presents —
what we're willing to take on as the

76
00:06:29,535 --> 00:06:31,722
party carrying the exposure.

77
00:06:31,722 --> 00:06:37,390
The goal of this training is to equip you
with the tools necessary to move deals

78
00:06:37,390 --> 00:06:42,627
